ed \n  on engine temperature, load, etc. The P0401 code means that OBD detected an \n  insufficient amount of EGR.\nSymptomsYou may notice drivability problems such as pinging (a.k.a. pre-ignition knock) when the engine is under load or the vehicle is at higher speeds. There may also be other symptoms.CausesA code P0401 most likely means one or more of the following has happened:The DPFE (differential pressure feedback EGR) sensor is faulty and needs \n    to be replacedThere is a blockage in the EGR (tube), most likely carbon buildupThe EGR valve is faultyThe EGR valve may not be opening due to a lack of vaccuumPossible SolutionsIn fixing this code, it is quite common for people to just replace the EGR valve only to have the OBD code return. The EGR valve is not always the culprit.Use a vacuum pump and pull the EGR valve open while monitoring engine RPM''s \n    & DPFE voltage. There should be a noticable difference in RPM''s with the \n    EGR openClean out the EGR valve and/or tubing to remove depositsCheck the voltage at the DPFE, compare to specified values (refer to a repair \n    manual for your specific model)Replace the DPFE sensor (with a good quality / OEM one)Replace the EGR valveRelated EGR codes:P0400,P0402,P0403,P0404,P0405,P0406,P0407,P0408,P0409Related  DTC DiscussionsRegister now to ask a question (free)P0401 on 97 Camry 4-cylGot the P0401 code for the first time a couple months back; disconnected battery to clear code, CEL went out and didn''t come back on for a few weeks (including a 1000 mile round trip). \n\nCame back on last weekend.  Same p0401, EGR flow insufficient code.\n\nGot a vacuum pump/gauge from AutoZone.  At i...P0401 on 96 grand prixI''m having trouble with a code p0401.....EGR flow insufficient on my 96 grand prix. I cleaned the intake and changed the EGR valve, but it still comes on . I''m hoping someone might have an idea for me. PLEASE HELP!!!...P0401 : EGR Flow Insufficient on 1996 Toyota CorollaHi Folks\n\nAny suggestions for